--------------------
One kind only: material **extracted at build time** from a tarball you supply.  No third-party
source file is checked into this repository.  What is checked in is the licence texts the
attribution requires, in `THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES/` — those are a condition of the licences rather
than an exception to the rule, and they are reproduced in full.

**Motorola 68040/68060 Support Packages** (FPSP, ISP).  Extracted at build time from a NetBSD
source tarball you supply.

Motorola's licence grants use, modification and distribution on two conditions: that the entire
notice is retained without alteration in any modified or redistributed version, and that modified
versions are clearly identified as such.  Both are met here:

* the notices are reproduced in full and unaltered in `THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES/`, one file per
  package;
* the same notices are assembled and linked into every kernel this repository builds
  (`copyright.s` / `copyright.S`), and the build fails if either file is missing;
* the three files in this repository that are modified versions under that licence --
  `src/fpsp_glue040.s`, `src/fpsp060_glue.s` and `src/fpsp060_head.s` -- each say so in their own
  header and point at the notice.

**Motorola's own skeleton files.**  The 060SP kit ships `fskeletn.s`, a skeleton the integrator is
expected to fill in, and two short sequences in `src/fpsp060_glue.s` follow it exactly because the
package's contract leaves no freedom there: the three-instruction prelude of the six IEEE
arithmetic exits (`fsave`, write `0x6000` into the saved status word, `frestore`), and the five
instructions of the BSUN exit that clear the FPSR NaN condition and discard the saved state.  Only
the destination differs -- ours dispatches to the stock SVR4 signal policy.  NetBSD's `fnetbsd.S`
is derived from the same skeleton and is identical in these places, which is why the source
comments name both; the attribution that matters here is Motorola's, and it travels with the
package as described above.

**NetBSD/m68k FPE** — extracted at build time, exactly as the Motorola packages are, and it is
not in this repository.  The soft-FPU lane compiles 25 files from `usr/src/sys/arch/m68k/fpe/`
of the same NetBSD tarball, plus four machine-ABI headers from it (`m68k/cpuframe.h`,
`m68k/fpreg.h`, `m68k/ieee.h`, `sys/ieee754.h`) that the emulator's own `#include`s reach for and
AMIX has no equivalent of.  `src/extract_fpe.sh` takes all twenty-nine out of the pinned tarball
into `build/fpe-src/` on every build: it verifies the tarball's sha256 first, then diffs the
extracted tree against a second fresh extraction, so an edit or an addition anywhere in that tree
stops the build instead of reaching a kernel.  `build/` is not tracked, so no file of theirs
appears in this repository or in its history.  Nothing in the tree is a modified version.  The
first-party compatibility headers the port had to write live in `src/fpe-compat/`, outside the
extracted space, and none of them is a copy of a NetBSD header.

