**NetBSD** was also used as a reference for architectural *behaviour* rather than as a source of
code, in places unrelated to the above:

* `wb040.s` -- the 68040 write-back realignment, where NetBSD's `m68040_writeback` documents how
  WB1D/WB2D are justified against WB3D;
* `fpu060.s` -- confirmation of a single fact, that the 68060 frame discriminator is at `frame+2`
  and not at byte zero (`switch_subr.s` tests `2(%a2)` on the 060, `(%a2)` otherwise). That file
  deliberately does **not** follow NetBSD's register ordering, and says so in a section written
  for the purpose.

NetBSD is BSD-licensed; attribution is given here and at each site in the source.

**Linux/m68k** was consulted for behaviour when establishing contracts (notably the 68060 MA
fault rounding and the write-back propagation model).  No code was copied.

The one place where interface text is reproduced
------------------------------------------------
`include-modelb/sys/param.h` and `include-modelb/sys/immu.h` are this port's page-geometry
override: the vendor's own headers with the page shift changed from 11 to 12, substituted into a
mirror sysroot so that C compiled into the kernel sees the same 4 KiB page the kernel uses.

Eleven one-line macros in them are identical to the originals -- `ptob`, `btop`, `btopr`,
`PAGEMASK`, `PAGEOFFSET`, `phystopfn`, `pfntophys`, `kvtopfn` and the `mmu_*` forms.  That is not
a shortcut: these macros are the kernel's compile-time ABI, code compiled against them has to
agree with the kernel bit for bit, and there is no second way to write "shift by the page shift"
that still interoperates.  What this port changed is the constant.

This is stated rather than buried because the rest of the repository claims to reproduce no
vendor text, and one exception that nobody mentions is worse than one exception that is argued
for.  `tools/check-verbatim.py` reports them on every run.

They could be generated at build time from the reader's own installed headers instead -- the same
patches-not-files pattern this project already uses for the AmigaOS loader.  See
`tools/check-verbatim.allow` for that option; it is open, not closed.

